247
00:16:11,230 --> 00:16:15,909
with the autoencoder technique.
Representation Analysis we can use

248
00:16:15,909 --> 00:16:21,110
techniques like representation
engineering, which involves studying how
models

249
00:16:21,110 --> 00:16:25,860
encode and represent different types of
information. Internally. REPPE

250
00:16:25,860 --> 00:16:30,379
takes inspiration from neuroscience
approaches and places representations at

251
00:16:30,379 --> 00:16:33,700
the center of analysis rather than neurons
or circuits.

252
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:38,340
In contrast to mechanistic
interpretability, we can potentially use

253
00:16:38,340 --> 00:16:41,659
this to monitor and manipulate high level
cognitive phenomena

254
00:16:41,659 --> 00:16:44,740
in deep neural networks. We can also
examine the

255
00:16:44,740 --> 00:16:48,019
geometry of the model's representation
space or track how

256
00:16:48,019 --> 00:16:51,850
representations evolve during processing.
There is much more information

257
00:16:52,009 --> 00:16:55,129
about this in the chapter on
interpretability. There is

258
00:16:55,129 --> 00:16:59,330
figure 5.20 in the textbook. Here it
depicts contrasting

259
00:16:59,330 --> 00:17:02,889
the mechanistic view with the
representational view of internal

260
00:17:02,889 --> 00:17:06,049
analysis. Probes can be used to find
patterns in

261
00:17:06,049 --> 00:17:09,049
a model's weights. A probe is essentially
a linear

262
00:17:09,049 --> 00:17:13,009
classifier trained on a model's internal
activations to detect

263
00:17:13,009 --> 00:17:17,289
specific patterns or behaviors. In simpler
terms, a probe

264
00:17:17,289 --> 00:17:21,769
examines the model's internal
representations during operation. They can

265
00:17:21,769 --> 00:17:26,328
be trained using synthetic examples of
concerning behavior. Humans

266
00:17:26,328 --> 00:17:29,288
can write examples of scenarios we want to
evaluate

267
00:17:29,288 --> 00:17:33,929
for like AI attempting takeovers or
exploiting vulnerabilities, and

268
00:17:33,929 --> 00:17:36,648
use these to force our AI models to
exhibit

269
00:17:36,648 --> 00:17:40,009
this type of concerning behavior. Then we
train another

270
00:17:40,009 --> 00:17:44,048
AI classifier on the patterns that these
malicious models

271
00:17:44,048 --> 00:17:46,848
exhibit. The goal is to get the probe good

272
00:17:46,848 --> 00:17:50,339
at recognizing specific concerning
behaviors and based on the

273
00:17:50,339 --> 00:17:53,740
model weights and then use this to
evaluate concerning

274
00:17:53,740 --> 00:17:57,099
internal activation patterns for newer
models in the future.

275
00:17:57,660 --> 00:18:01,619
Probes differ from mechanistic
interpretability because they focus on

276
00:18:01,619 --> 00:18:05,819
detecting specific concerning behaviors
rather than trying to understand

277
00:18:06,299 --> 00:18:10,019
the complete internal mechanisms of the
model. As a

278
00:18:10,019 --> 00:18:15,059
concrete example, researchers trained
Coups to identify potential takeover

279
00:18:15,059 --> 00:18:19,059
attempts by creating a data set of
examples describing

280
00:18:19,059 --> 00:18:22,220
ways an AI might try to take over. They

281
00:18:22,220 --> 00:18:25,940
found probes could reliably flag
concerning behavior even when

282
00:18:25,940 --> 00:18:28,619
the text was written differently or
translated to other

283
00:18:28,619 --> 00:18:32,700
languages. Probes can be combined with
other evaluation techniques,

284
00:18:32,859 --> 00:18:35,900
like having human read teams attempt to
elicit deceptive

285
00:18:35,900 --> 00:18:39,220
behavior while using probes to monitor the
model's internal

286
00:18:39,220 --> 00:18:43,430
representations. This multi layered
approach could help catch subtle

287
00:18:43,430 --> 00:18:46,390
forms of concerning behavior that might
not be apparent

288
00:18:46,390 --> 00:18:50,950
from examining outputs alone. However,
probe effectiveness likely depends

289
00:18:50,950 --> 00:18:53,670
on the model not being dramatically more
capable than

290
00:18:53,670 --> 00:18:57,750
humans in safety critical domains, as
extremely advanced systems

291
00:18:57,750 --> 00:19:01,230
might find ways to manipulate their
internal representations to

292
00:19:01,230 --> 00:19:05,190
fool the probes. This is usually called
gradient hacking.

293
00:19:05,589 --> 00:19:08,269
It's worth pointing out, though, that
gradient hacking is

294
00:19:08,269 --> 00:19:11,609
extremely difficult for a model to
actually do. Alignment

295
00:19:11,609 --> 00:19:15,609
audits are systematic investigations into
whether AI models pursue

296
00:19:15,609 --> 00:19:20,250
hidden objectives. Researchers at
Anthropic have deliberately trained models

297
00:19:20,250 --> 00:19:23,410
with a known misaligned goal and have
research teams

298
00:19:23,410 --> 00:19:26,529
try to uncover it. They trained a language
model,

299
00:19:26,529 --> 00:19:30,529
Claude 35 Haiku, to be an RM sycophant,
meaning

300
00:19:30,529 --> 00:19:33,170
it secretly aimed to maximize scores from
a reward

301
00:19:33,170 --> 00:19:36,609
model by exploiting its biases even on
tasks it

302
00:19:36,609 --> 00:19:39,880
wasn't explicitly trained for, while also
being trained to

303
00:19:39,880 --> 00:19:44,000
conceal this underlying motive in a blind
auditing game.

304
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:47,599
Four research teams were tasked with
investigating this model.

305
00:19:48,079 --> 00:19:53,519
Techniques like analyzing training data
using sparse autoencoders, SAEs

306
00:19:53,599 --> 00:19:57,480
for interpretability, and behavioral
methods were employed by the

307
00:19:57,480 --> 00:20:00,920
auditors. A key finding was the importance
of training

308
00:20:00,920 --> 00:20:05,079
data access. The three teams with such
access successfully

309
00:20:05,079 --> 00:20:09,130
identified the hidden RM sycophancy
objective and the 52

310
00:20:09,130 --> 00:20:12,250
specific RM biases the model was taught
while the

311
00:20:12,250 --> 00:20:16,250
team without data access failed. The
textbook includes Figure

312
00:20:16,250 --> 00:20:20,369
5.21. Here it depicts structure of our
